<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Provisional Government of Judea</h1><p class="st-meta">66 CE – 70 CE · capital Jerusalem</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The rebel government of the First Jewish Revolt, which minted its own coins and held Jerusalem against Rome until the city and Temple fell in 70.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Riots in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-caesarea">Caesarea</a> and the plundering of the Temple treasury by the procurator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gessius-florus">Florus</a> led to open revolt in 66. After rebels routed the Syrian governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cestius-gallus">Cestius Gallus</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-beth-horon-66">Beth Horon</a>, an assembly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> appointed the former high priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ananus-ben-ananus">Ananus ben Ananus</a> and Joseph ben Gurion to lead it and sent commanders to the districts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> among them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The government struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> shekels dated by years of the revolt and inscribed '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> the Holy', a declaration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. Its moderate priestly leadership was overthrown in 68 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zealots">Zealots</a> allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idumaeans">Idumaeans</a>, who killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ananus-ben-ananus">Ananus</a>. Jerusalem then split between rival factions under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-gischala">John of Gischala</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bar-giora">Simon bar Giora</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleazar-ben-simon">Eleazar ben Simon</a>, who fought each other and burned food stores.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Vespasian subdued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> in 67 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus">Titus</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 70, destroying the city and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> that summer. The last strongholds, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masada">Masada</a>, fell by 73-74. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judea</a> became a separate province with a legion stationed in the ruins of Jerusalem.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/provisional-government-of-judea.jpg" alt="Provisional Government of Judea" width="320" height="156"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shekel_from_third_year_of_the_first_Jewish-Roman_war.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>66 CE – 70 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jerusalem</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ananus ben Ananus, John of Gischala, Simon bar Giora</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Judaism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Aramaic, Hebrew, Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Israel, Palestine</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>First Jewish Revolt government</dd></dl></aside></div>
